 ## Background
-The concept of a total loss function in differentiable volume rendering emerged with the advent of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and subsequent variants. Early works like NeRF used primarily photometric loss [S5]. As the field evolved, researchers introduced additional terms to address limitations such as floaters, geometric inaccuracies, and training instability. Key developments include the introduction of Eikonal loss for SDFs [S6, S11], sparsity and opacity regularization [S2, S5], and advanced techniques like dual-buffer loss for path tracing [S18, unofficial] and curriculum learning [S13]. The total loss function has become a standard component in modern radiance field optimization pipelines, adapting to various representations including implicit fields, Gaussian splatting, and voxel grids [S1, S2, S8][S15, S19, unofficial].
+The concept of a total loss function in differentiable volume rendering emerged with the advent of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and subsequent variants [G]. Early works like NeRF used primarily photometric loss [S5]. As the field evolved, researchers introduced additional terms to address limitations such as floaters, geometric inaccuracies, and training instability [G]. Key developments include the introduction of Eikonal loss for SDFs [S6, S11], sparsity and opacity regularization [S2, S5], and advanced techniques like dual-buffer loss for path tracing [S18, unofficial] and curriculum learning [S16]. The total loss function has become a standard component in modern radiance field optimization pipelines, adapting to various representations including implicit fields, Gaussian splatting, and voxel grids [S1, S2, S8][S15, S19, unofficial].
 
